---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { profileLinks } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy — Stefano Carlo Lambertenghi"
  description="What this website stores, which outside services it contacts and when, and how to delete or refuse it."
>
  <section class="page-hero page-hero-light">
    <div class="page-hero-inner container-wide">
      <div>
        <h1 class="page-title">Privacy.</h1>
        <p class="page-hero-copy">
          No analytics, no advertising, no cookies. This page lists everything the site stores in
          your browser and every outside service your browser contacts, and when.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-tight-top">
    <div class="container privacy">
      <p class="privacy-updated">Last updated 4 November 2026</p>

      <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
      <p>
        Stefano Carlo Lambertenghi runs this personal website. For any question about your data,
        write to{' '}
        <a class="js-email-link" href="#" data-email-user={profileLinks.emailUser} data-email-domain={profileLinks.emailDomain}>me by email</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Hosting</h2>
      <p>
        The site is served by GitHub Pages (GitHub, Inc.). Like any web server, GitHub receives
        your IP address and request details when you open a page and logs visitors' IP addresses
        for security purposes. I have no access to these logs. See the{' '}
        <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub General Privacy Statement</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What is stored in your browser</h2>
      <p>
        The site sets no cookies. Only the garage game (Just for fun) keeps a few things in your
        browser's local storage, so a returning driver has something to beat:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>your best lap or run time, per track and vehicle;</li>
        <li>the replay of that lap, used for the ghost car;</li>
        <li>up to three streets you loaded from the map, so they open again without a new request;</li>
        <li>whether you agreed to load maps from OpenStreetMap (see below).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of it contains anything about you, and none of it is ever sent anywhere: it stays on
        your device. It is only written when you use the garage. You can delete it with the{' '}
        <strong>Clear saved data</strong> button in the garage, or by clearing this site's data in
        your browser settings.
      </p>

      <h2>Outside services, and when they are contacted</h2>
      <p>
        Some features need data from other services. When your browser contacts them, they
        receive your IP address, your browser's standard request details (such as its user agent
        and the referring page), and the request itself. None of them are contacted on page load
        without a reason listed here.
      </p>

      <h3>OpenStreetMap map tiles</h3>
      <p>
        Used by the journey map on the About page and the street picker in the garage. They load
        only after you choose to show a map; that choice is remembered in your browser and
        covers both maps. The tiles come from the OpenStreetMap Foundation's servers, which
        receive the map area you look at. See the{' '}
        <a href="https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">OpenStreetMap Foundation Privacy Policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Overpass API</h3>
      <p>
        The garage's "Any street" track is built from OpenStreetMap data, which the site cannot
        hold for the whole world. When you press <strong>Load this route</strong>, your browser
        asks the public Overpass API at{' '}
        <a href="https://overpass-api.de/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">overpass-api.de</a>{' '}
        for the streets and buildings around the two points you picked. It receives that area's
        coordinates and your IP address. Without it, that one track cannot work; every other part
        of the site works without it.
      </p>

      <h3>YouTube</h3>
      <p>
        Talk recordings on the Demos and Teaching pages are embedded from YouTube in its
        privacy-enhanced mode (youtube-nocookie.com). The player loads as you scroll near it,
        and from then on Google receives your IP address and request details. See{' '}
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Google's Privacy Policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
      <p>
        You can refuse the maps: the rest of the site, including every other garage track, works
        without them. To withdraw a map choice you made, use <strong>Clear saved data</strong> in
        the garage or clear this site's data in your browser. Because I do not collect or keep
        personal data myself, requests about data held by GitHub, the OpenStreetMap Foundation,
        the Overpass API or Google go to those providers. Under the GDPR you also have the right
        to lodge a complaint with a data protection supervisory authority.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .privacy {
    max-width: 44rem;
  }

  .privacy-updated {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .privacy h2 {
    margin: 2.8rem 0 0.9rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.2rem);
    font-weight: 560;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .privacy h3 {
    margin: 1.8rem 0 0.6rem;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .privacy p,
  .privacy li {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .privacy p + p {
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
  }

  .privacy ul {
    margin: 0.9rem 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    list-style: disc;
  }

  .privacy li::marker {
    color: var(--signal);
  }

  .privacy li + li {
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
  }

  .privacy a {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }
</style>

